<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hafez al-Assad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hafez al-Assad was a Syrian air force officer and Ba'ath party leader who seized power in 1970 and was President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1971 until his death in 2000. He built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> resting on the army, security services and his Alawite community, crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> uprising of 1982 and kept Syrian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad.jpg" alt="Hafez al-Assad" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Syria · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hafez_al-Assad_official_portrait_(precise_crop)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hafez al-Assad was born on 6 October 1930 in Qardaha, a town in northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, into a poor Alawite peasant family of the Kalbiyya tribe. He was the ninth son of his father Ali al-Assad, who had eleven children. In Sunni-dominated Latakia, where he faced anti-Alawite bias, he became the first in his family to attend high school, and he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a> in 1946. He wanted to study medicine, but his father could not pay, so in 1950 he entered the Homs Military Academy and then the flying school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. In 1955 he graduated as a lieutenant in the Syrian Air Force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Assad, Salah Jadid, Muhammad Umran and others established the Ba'ath Military Committee, and he was a key participant in the 1963 coup that brought the party to power. He was appointed commander of the Syrian Arab Air Force. After a second coup in February 1966 he became Minister of Defense under Jadid, who gave him de facto control of the army. Four years later Assad initiated a third coup, ousting the quasi-Marxist Jadid, and became prime minister in 1970. A referendum approved him as president on 12 March 1971.</p><p>In power he dropped Jadid's policy of exporting socialist revolution and allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> while keeping ties with some Western European and Gulf states. Syria fought Israel in the 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur-war">Yom Kippur War</a>, and in 1976 it invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> with 30,000 troops. Syria remained a one-party system, with decision-making placed under the president's control. From 1973 to 1976 an Arabization campaign known as the Arab Belt deported around 140,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> from 332 villages in the north-east. His regime crushed an Islamist uprising led by the Syrian Muslim Brotherhood through crackdowns that culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> massacre, in which two thirds of the city was destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Assad's eldest son Bassel, his chosen heir, died in a car crash in 1994, and he turned to his younger son Bashar, then a medical student abroad. He demoted officials who opposed the succession. Assad died in office in June 2000 and Bashar succeeded him. Hafez al-Assad exerted considerable influence on the politics of the region; the New York Times wrote that no lasting peace could hold without him, but none could be negotiated with him either.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hafez al-Assad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the union all Syrian parties ceased open activity, while a secret Military Committee of Ba'athist officers, Muhammad Umran, Salah Jadid and <b>Hafez al-Assad</b> among them, prepared for its collapse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The coup of 1966 led by Salah Jadid and <b>Hafez al-Assad</b> overthrew the old guard of Aflaq and Bitar, and many Syrian Ba'athist leaders defected to Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/baathism">Ba'athism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qardaha, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.46,36.06&amp;year=1975" aria-label="Qardaha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5462 882 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hafez al-Assad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5912 1152h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5912 1152h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.46,36.06&amp;year=1975">What was here in 1975? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
